 ],
 "literal": "My father! May you live long, O Ātaṉuṅka! My / heart, those who open, they will see you indeed. / You, if I forget, the time of forgetting / is when my life from my body separates, and even then / myself if I forget, I will forget. O victorious spear-holder! / Touching the sky, with tall umbrellas and banner-bearing chariots, the Mauryas, / whose strong-spoked wheels, as they rolled, cut down / the world's mountain pass, the opening in the rock, where stands / like the wide-spreading sun-disc, every day / supporting many people, you who have undertaken the path of virtue!",
 "translation": "My lord, may you live long, O Ātaṉuṅka! If anyone were to open my heart, they would see only you inside. If I were ever to forget you, that time of forgetting would only be when my life departs from my body. Even then, I would forget myself before I forget you. O lord of the victorious spear! Like the wide-spreading sun that shines daily over the mountain pass carved through the rocks by the rolling, strong-spoked wheels of the banner-bearing chariots of the Mauryas with their sky-touching tall umbrellas, you stand every day, steadfast in the path of virtue, supporting and protecting many.",
 "modern": "என் தந்தையே! ஆதனுங்கனே! நீ டூழி வாழ்க! என் நெஞ்சத்தைத் திறந்து பார்ப்பவர்கள் உன்னையே காண்பார்கள். உன்னை நான் மறப்பதாக இருந்தால், என் உயிர் உடம்பிலிருந்து பிரியும் அந்த இறுதி நேரத்தில் மட்டுமே அது நிகழும்; அப்போதும் கூட, என்னை நான் மறந்தால் மட்டுமே உன்னை மறப்பேன். வெற்றி தரும் வேலை ஏந்தியவனே! வானத்தைத் தொடும் உயர்ந்த குடைகளையும் கொடிகளையும் கொண்ட தேர்களையுடைய மௌரியர்கள், தங்களுடைய வலிமையான கதிர்களைக் கொண்ட சக்கரங்கள் உருண்டோடி வெட்டிய உலகத்தின் மலைப் பாதையாகிய பிளவிலே நிலைபெற்று விளங்கும், அகன்ற இடத்தையுடைய சூரிய மண்டலத்தைப் போல, நாள்தோறும் பலரையும் காக்கும் அறநெறியை ஏற்றுக்கொண்ட அறத்துறையாக விளங்குபவன் நீயே!",
 "explanation": "In this poem, the poet Kaḷḷil Āttiraiyaṉār addresses his patron chieftain, Ātaṉuṅkaṉ, in the Pāṭāṇ tiṇai (praise of a patron). The poet expresses absolute devotion, declaring that if anyone were to open his chest, they would find only the patron inside. He asserts that he could only forget Ātaṉuṅkaṉ at the moment of death, and even then, he would forget his own identity before forgetting his benefactor. To describe the patron's constant generosity, the poet uses a grand historical allusion to the Mauryas, whose chariot wheels cut a pass through the southern mountains. Just as the sun shines daily over that mountain pass, the patron stands steadfast every day, protecting and supporting many. Key words include 'neñcam tiṟappōr' (those who open the heart), 'maṟakkuveṉ' (I will forget), and 'aṟatturai' (path of virtue).",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
